  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Testing Weather Data Input**\n\nI\'m verifying the application\'s capacity to process the user\'s hardcoded weather data for Y T\303\275 on 24/02. I\'m focusing on the provided surface and upper-air temperature readings along with the 850 hPa wind data. Specifically, I\'m examining how the application handles `T_surf`, `Td_surf`, `T_850`, `T_700`, and `Wind_850` input.\n\n\n**Refining Code Modification Strategy**\n\nI\'m now honing in on a more direct approach. Considering I can\'t interact with the application directly, I\'ll revise `services/geminiService.ts`. The aim is to conditionally inject the user\'s hardcoded weather values directly into the Gemini prompt whenever \"Y T\303\275\" and the specified date are entered, effectively bypassing the need for manual UI manipulation. This should allow me to simulate the testing scenario they described, even though I can\'t physically test.\n\n\n**Interpreting Data and Calculations**\n\nNow, I\'m analyzing the implications of the weather data provided to test the app. I\'m focusing on calculating the Lifting Condensation Level (LCL), considering Y T\303\275\'s altitude and the provided temperature readings. I am assuming T_surf is at the base. I\'m trying to decide if the calculated LCL is relative to Y T\303\275\'s elevation or a standard base elevation. My primary goal is to determine if the cloud base height of 2025m is correct. I am analyzing algorithms in `geminiService.ts` to see what is applicable to Module 1.\n\n\n**Analyzing Inversion Layer**\n\nI\'m now investigating the inversion layer\'s impact on cloud top estimation. Given T_850 at 12\302\260C and T_surf at 15\302\260C, I\'m confirming a significant inversion. This challenges the expected lapse rate where temperature decreases with altitude, suggesting a cap on cloud development. I\'m focusing on the altitude discrepancy between provided surface and 850hPa temperature data, and how this relates to inversion height and cloud top potential, considering that cloud is trapped below warmer air.\n\n\n**Determining Cloud Top Constraints**\n\nI\'m now determining cloud top constraints based on the provided temperature inversion, specifically analyzing the altitude difference between Y T\303\275 (1900m) and the temperature at 850 hPa (1500m), along with their temperatures to understand if the cloud will fluctuate (delta H <= 200m) or is static (delta H > 300m). I will adjust the prompt generation to inject the Y T\303\275 data. I aim to create a test case that focuses the analysis on this specific inversion profile, calculating the delta and verifying cloud top predictions.\n\n\n**Implementing Data Injection Logic**\n\nI\'m now implementing the injection logic within `analyzeWeatherData` to include the hardcoded data whenever the location is \"Y T\303\275\". I\'m adding a conditional check for the location name and appending the provided data block to the prompt, which will allow me to test and generate the cloud top predictions. I will now integrate this within `geminiService.ts` to reflect the testing. I will test for the exact phrase \"Y T\303\275\".\n\n\n**Finalizing Prompt Injection**\n\nI\'ve finalized the prompt injection logic. To keep the simulation easily testable, I\'m sticking with a check for \"Y T\303\275\" (case-insensitive). I\'ve also incorporated a detailed hardcoded data block directly into the prompt to replace any other data on 24/02. The prompt now includes explicit instructions to the AI on how to handle and calculate cloud-top predictions based on the hardcoded data.\n\n\n**Refining Injection Prompt**\n\nI\'m now refining the prompt injection to specify the hardcoded data\'s date (\"24/02\"). I\'m ensuring the AI correctly interprets this specific date in its cloud-top calculations. I will then integrate this new prompt format within `geminiService.ts`. I also need to make sure the app works when \"Y T\303\275\" is in a range of dates, and not just 24/02, in case the user specified the wrong dates in the UI. I can hardcode a date range if necessary.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "b14af196-4fe9-409b-a855-25589600451c"
      name: "edit_file"
      arguments_json: "{\"Instruction\":\"Inject hardcoded test data for \'Y T\303\275\' into the prompt logic within analyzeWeatherData function.\",\"ReplacementContent\":\"  let terrainContext = \\\"\\\";\\n  if (matchedPreset && matchedPreset.elevation_profile) {\\n    terrainContext = `\\n    **KNOWN_LOCATIONS_LIBRARY (D\341\273\256 LI\341\273\206U \304\220\341\273\212A H\303\214NH \304\220\303\203 X\303\201C TH\341\273\260C):**\\n    \304\220\303\242y l\303\240 d\341\273\257 li\341\273\207u chu\341\272\251n c\341\273\247a h\341\273\207 th\341\273\221ng cho \304\221\341\273\213a \304\221i\341\273\203m n\303\240y.\\n    ${JSON.stringify(matchedPreset.elevation_profile)}\\n    `;\\n  }\\n\\n  // [TESTING INJECTION] - Hardcoded data for Y Ty testing\\n  let hardcodedDataInjection = \\\"\\\";\\n  if (normalizedInput.includes(\\\"y ty\\\")) {\\n      hardcodedDataInjection = `\\n      [TESTING_SCENARIO_ACTIVE]\\n      Ng\306\260\341\273\235i d\303\271ng \304\221ang y\303\252u c\341\272\247u ch\341\272\241y th\341\273\255 nghi\341\273\207m (Simulation Mode) v\341\273\233i b\341\273\231 d\341\273\257 li\341\273\207u c\341\273\251ng sau cho ng\303\240y 24/02. \\n      B\341\272\240N B\341\272\256T BU\341\273\230C PH\341\272\242I D\303\231NG S\341\273\220 LI\341\273\206U N\303\200Y \304\220\341\273\202 T\303\215NH TO\303\201N CHO NG\303\200Y 24/02, B\341\273\216 QUA D\341\273\256 LI\341\273\206U SEARCH CHO NG\303\200Y N\303\200Y.\\n      \\n      [HARD_DATA]\\n      - Location Altitude: 1900m (Y T\303\275)\\n      - T_surf (1900m): 15\302\260C\\n      - Td_surf (1900m): 14\302\260C\\n      - T_850 (1500m): 12\302\260C\\n      - T_700 (3000m): 5\302\260C\\n      - Wind_850: 8 km/h (\304\220\303\264ng Nam)\\n      \\n      H\303\203Y \303\201P D\341\273\244NG MODULE 6 \304\220\341\273\202 X\303\201C \304\220\341\273\212NH TR\341\272\240NG TH\303\201I M\303\202Y T\341\272\240I \304\220\341\273\230 CAO 1900M.\\n      L\306\260u \303\275: T_850 (12\302\260C) < T_surf (15\302\